      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "9e9af86d-46a6-505f-b496-a71541348c34",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Medhatithi Gautama, Pāṇini, Gotama, Akṣapāda Gautama, Nagarjuna, Dignāga\n- Organizations: Vaisheshika school, Nyaya school\n- Products: Nyāya Sūtras\n- Concepts: anviksiki, Sanskrit grammar, atomism, inference, tetralemma, reason, logic, epistemology, metaphysics, hetu vidyā\n\nFacts:\n- Medhatithi Gautama's anviksiki is dated to about the 7th century BCE in the development of Indian logic.\n- Pāṇini's Sanskrit grammar rules are dated to about the 5th century BCE in the development of Indian logic.\n- The Vaisheshika school's analysis of atomism is dated to about the 2nd century BCE in the development of Indian logic.\n- Gotama, founder of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy, analyzed inference around the 2nd century.\n- Nagarjuna's tetralemma is dated to about the 2nd century CE in the development of Indian logic.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras is an ancient Indian Sanskrit text composed by Akṣapāda Gautama and is the foundational text of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras consists of five books, each with two chapters, for a total of 528 aphoristic sutras on reason, logic, epistemology, and metaphysics.\n- Dignāga lived circa 480 to 540 CE and was an Indian Buddhist scholar and one of the Buddhist founders of Indian logic, also called hetu vidyā.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "6e4827a2-3ba5-558d-83f4-ce4b52ce8c82",
        "text": "Page: Indian logic. Sentence: The development of Indian logic dates back to the \"anviksiki\" of Medhatithi Gautama (c. 7th century BCE) the Sanskrit grammar rules of Pāṇini (c. 5th century BCE); the Vaisheshika school's analysis of atomism (c. 2nd century BCE); the analysis of inference by Gotama (c. 2nd century), founder of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy; and the tetralemma of Nagarjuna (c. 2nd century CE).\nPage: Nyāya Sūtras. Sentence: The Nyāya Sūtras is an ancient Indian Sanskrit text composed by Akṣapāda Gautama , and the foundational text of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy.\nPage: Nyāya Sūtras. Sentence: The text consists of five books, with two chapters in each book, with a cumulative total of 528 aphoristic sutras, about rules of reason, logic, epistemology and metaphysics.\nPage: Dignāga. Sentence: Dignāga (c. 480 – c. 540 CE) was an Indian Buddhist scholar and one of the Buddhist founders of Indian logic (\"hetu vidyā\").",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
